 \subsection{Simulink SNS Library and Reflex Topology}
 
-The Simulink tool is a block library, built so that synthetic nervous systems can also be assembled and inspected in a standard controls-engineering environment. Its seven core component blocks are a non-spiking (graded) neuron, a graded chemical synapse, a spiking leaky integrate-and-fire neuron, Ia and Ib afferent encoders, muscle activation dynamics, and a generic BPA force block. The blocks implement the same neuron and synapse equations used by the SNS-Toolbox networks of the preceding subsection, and their inventory is drawn in Figure~\ref{fig:simulink_sns_implementation} (top), which also shows the internal implementation of the non-spiking neuron block as a native Simulink export (bottom). The full library adds a synapse-sum junction for neurons receiving more than six synaptic inputs, three diameter-specific BPA force elements (\qtylist{10;20;40}{\mm}) implementing the fitted force surfaces of Chapter~\ref{ch:methods}, and a Hill-type biological muscle element, for twelve blocks before the spiking additions of Section~\ref{sec:spiking_methods}. The library and its demonstrations run in base Simulink without additional toolboxes; the block icons derive their excitatory and inhibitory symbols automatically from the sign of each synapse, in a color-blind-safe palette, and the publication figures regenerate from scripts.
+The Simulink tool is a block library, built so that synthetic nervous systems can also be assembled and inspected in a standard controls-engineering environment. Its seven core component blocks are a non-spiking (graded) neuron, a graded chemical synapse, a spiking leaky integrate-and-fire neuron, Ia and Ib afferent encoders, muscle activation dynamics, and a generic BPA force block. The blocks implement the same neuron and synapse equations used by the SNS-Toolbox networks of the preceding subsection, and their inventory is drawn in Figure~\ref{fig:simulink_sns_implementation} (top), which also shows a functional schematic of the non-spiking neuron implementation (bottom). The full library adds a synapse-sum junction for neurons receiving more than six synaptic inputs, three diameter-specific BPA force elements (\qtylist{10;20;40}{\mm}) implementing the fitted force surfaces of Chapter~\ref{ch:methods}, and a Hill-type biological muscle element, for twelve blocks before the spiking additions of Section~\ref{sec:spiking_methods}. The library and its demonstrations run in base Simulink without additional toolboxes; the block icons derive their excitatory and inhibitory symbols automatically from the sign of each synapse, in a color-blind-safe palette, and the publication figures regenerate from scripts.
 
 The library's fundamental demonstrations are two basic reflex pathways. In the first, the core blocks are assembled into a reduced-order knee-reflex model with antagonist actuation (Figure~\ref{fig:simulink_reflex_topology}): Ia stretch feedback excites the motor neuron of the stretched muscle and inhibits that of its antagonist (reciprocal inhibition), while Ib force feedback inhibits the motor neuron of the muscle that generated the force (autogenic inhibition). In the second, an arm holds a beverage container while a pour loads it, with and without the stretch reflex enabled. Between them, the two demonstrations show the library reproducing the canonical spinal reflex pathways at the scale where their mechanisms are visible; their quantitative results are reported in Section~\ref{sec:sns_simulink_demos} beside their spiking twins. To couple the controller to detailed mechanical CAD, the SolidWorks assembly is exported and imported into Simscape Multibody. The URDF import path is verified on a trial knee assembly, and the fuller Multibody-Link XML import of the knee test assembly exposes a documented defect: the exporter drops the SolidWorks hinge mates as unsupported, so the knee degree of freedom arrives welded, and the defined fix is to re-mate each hinge as a concentric and coincident pair before re-export. The tendon hardware is modeled in CAD but not yet inserted into the assembly. The demonstration model retains provisional mechanical parameters, the generic BPA force block remains a placeholder, and the saved runs are therefore not used to establish quantitative knee-regulation performance.
 
